<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timbuktu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Timbuktu is a city near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, at the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It became a major centre of the trans-Saharan trade and of Islamic learning under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and reached its height under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, when its mosques and scholars were famous throughout the Islamic world. Its prosperity declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr user: Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile https://www.flickr.com/people/3059349393/ · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
